General elections were held in [[Guatemala]] on 16 June 2019, to elect the president, [[Congress of Guatemala|Congress]] and local councils. A second round of the presidential elections was held on 11 August 2019, since no candidate won a majority in the first round. [[Alejandro Giammattei]] won the election in the second round of voting.

Incumbent President [[Jimmy Morales]] was constitutionally barred from running for a second four-year term.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.prensalibre.com/guatemala/politica/jimmy-morales-dice-que-le-gustaria-quedarse-gobernando-por-otro-periodo|title=Jimmy Morales dice que le gustaría gobernar otro período|date=7 March 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.efe.com/efe/america/politica/el-presidente-de-guatemala-dice-que-le-gustaria-tener-otro-periodo-gobierno/20000035-3546202|title=El presidente de Guatemala dice que le gustaría tener otro período de Gobierno|accessdate=8 March 2023}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The [[president of Guatemala]] is elected using the [[two-round system]].<ref name=EG/>

The 160 members of Congress are elected by two methods; 130 are elected from 22 multi-member constituencies based on the [[Departments of Guatemala|departments]], with the remaining 31 elected from a single nationwide constituency. Seats are elected using [[closed list]] [[proportional representation]], with seats allocated using the [[D'Hondt method]].<ref name=EG>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2419/ Congress of the Republic] IFES</ref>

==Candidates==
[[Thelma Aldana]]'s candidature was rejected by authorities in April 2019 on the grounds of alleged corruption cases concerning her, although no evidence was presented. She denied the accusations and attributes them to what she calls the "pact of the corrupt", composed of politicians and business leaders of the country. As former attorney general, she had uncovered several major corruption cases.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.lefigaro.fr/flash-actu/guatemala-la-candidature-de-l-ex-procureure-rejetee-20190402|title = Guatemala: La candidature de l'ex-procureure rejetée|date = 2 April 2019}}</ref> Aldana appealed the ruling, but the appeal was rejected in May 2019.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/the_americas/guatemala-awaits-ruling-can-popular-anti-corruption-crusader-run-for-president/2019/05/14/8ec59356-7516-11e9-a7bf-c8a43b84ee31_story.html?noredirect=on|title=Guatemala court rules popular anti-corruption crusader ineligible to run for president|last=Cuffe|first=Sandra|date=15 May 2019|newspaper=Washington Post|access-date=16 May 2019}}</ref>

The candidature of [[Zury Ríos]], daughter of Guatemalan dictator from 1982 to 1983 [[Efraín Ríos Montt]], was rejected by authorities in May 2019 on the grounds that the country's constitution bars close relatives of coup leaders from serving as president.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-48266200|title=Guatemala ex-dictator's daughter Zury Ríos barred from presidency|date=14 May 2019|work=BBC News}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-guatemala-politics/guatemala-court-bars-ex-dictators-daughter-from-presidential-race-idUSKCN1SK06U|title=Guatemala court bars ex-dictator's daughter from presidential race|date=13 May 2019|work=Reuters}}</ref>

In April 2019, centre-right candidate Mario Estrada was arrested. He is accused by the American authorities of having made a deal with the [[Sinaloa cartel]], from which he allegedly obtained between 10 and 12 million dollars for his election campaign, and of having ordered the murder of rival candidates.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.ouest-france.fr/monde/guatemala/guatemala-un-candidat-la-presidentielle-arrete-pour-complot-avec-un-cartel-6313938|title=Guatemala. Un candidat à la présidentielle arrêté pour complot avec un cartel|date=18 April 2019|work=Ouest France|access-date=8 March 2023|language=fr|agency=Reuters}}</ref>

